### 15\. Security, operational integrity and failure prevention

EP should be designed around the assumption that individual components can fail.

No single database, validator, Oracle, autonomous agent, or storage device should be treated as infallible.

### 15.1 Principal threats

| Threat                 | Proposed mitigation                         |
| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| Data manipulation      | Cryptographic integrity checks              |
| Unauthorized execution | BAI permission boundaries                   |
| Malicious validators   | BFT consensus                               |
| Storage failure        | Replication and erasure coding              |
| Conflicting updates    | BBE reconciliation                          |
| Incorrect Oracle data  | Independent source verification             |
| Credential compromise  | Key rotation and revocation                 |
| Resource exhaustion    | Quotas and execution leases                 |
| Software compromise    | Signed builds and verified deployment       |
| Clock drift            | Trusted time sources and drift monitoring   |
| Automation errors      | Human escalation and rollback               |
| Financial misuse       | Transaction limits and independent approval |

### 15.2 Independent integrity controls

I recommend separating four forms of integrity:

1\. Mathematical integrity

Ensures calculations, numerical measurements, and transformations are correct according to defined rules.

2\. Information integrity

Ensures stored or transmitted information has not been altered without detection.

3\. Operational integrity

Ensures system activities occur within approved permissions and execution boundaries.

4\. Epistemic integrity

Ensures interpretations and conclusions remain supported by identifiable evidence, appropriate context, and transparent reasoning.

These four controls should produce independent verification results.

Their reconciliation becomes an EP-wide quality determination.

